{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Happy are you, etc. saved by HaShem.</b> The meaning is that all the nations — their princes fight for them, as is known, and in this there is grounds for concern that the prince opposing them might overpower them, unless HaShem strikes him, as He said (Exodus 14:30) ״וַיַּרְא יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת מִצְרַיִם מֵת״ etc., and our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 21:5) that HaShem showed them the prince of Egypt dead before them — which is not so for Israel; happy are they, for their salvation is in HaShem, for He Himself in His glory fights for them, and in this their sword is held high and stands without fail." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in saying ״saved by HaShem״ — meaning that the King Himself is saved by His salvation, as He said (Deuteronomy 30:3) ״וְשָׁב ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֶת שְׁבוּתְךָ״, and they of blessed memory said (Megillah 29a): it was not said ״וְהֵשִׁיב״ but rather ״וְשָׁב״ — happy is He, etc. And this is what He says: ״אַשְׁרֶיךָ מִי כָמוֹךָ״ — that you are a people saved together with HaShem, who is the shield of your help, and who is the sword of your pride; for there is no sword as great as the sword of HaShem when He draws it from its sheath."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:31.539336350Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}